DATA={'III/23': {'intro': 'Weak boundary-value problems replace pointwise differential equations by integral identities tested against admissible functions. This is the natural formulation for rough coefficients, Sobolev solutions, and variational existence theory.', 'sections': [('Strong and weak formulations', 'A strong solution satisfies the PDE pointwise; a weak solution satisfies an integrated identity obtained by multiplying by test functions and integrating by parts.'), ('Energy spaces', 'Sobolev spaces such as $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ encode square-integrable weak gradients and homogeneous boundary conditions.'), ('Bilinear forms', 'Second-order elliptic equations are represented by bilinear forms such as $a(u,v)=\\int_\\Omega A\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v+cuv$.'), ('Linear functionals', 'Forcing terms act as continuous linear functionals on the chosen energy space.'), ('Coercivity', 'A coercive estimate $a(v,v)\\ge c\\|v\\|_V^2$ supplies control of the solution norm.'), ('Weak boundary conditions', 'Dirichlet conditions are built into $H_0^1$; Neumann data typically arise through boundary terms in integration by parts.'), ('Galerkin approximation', 'Finite-dimensional subspaces convert the weak problem into linear algebra and support constructive approximation.'), ('Uniqueness by energy', 'Testing the difference of two solutions against itself often proves uniqueness from positivity or coercivity.')], 'theorems': [('Lax--Milgram theorem', 'If $V$ is a Hilbert space, $a$ is bounded and coercive, and $F\\in V^*$, then there is a unique $u\\in V$ with $a(u,v)=F(v)$ for all $v\\in V$.', 'Use Riesz representation to write $a(u,v)=\\langle Au,v\\rangle$ for a bounded operator $A$. Coercivity makes $A$ bounded below, hence injective with closed range; orthogonality to the range would contradict coercivity for the adjoint, so the range is all of $V$.'), ('Energy uniqueness', 'If $a$ is coercive and $a(u,v)=0$ for all $v$, then $u=0$.', 'Choose $v=u$. Then $0=a(u,u)\\ge c\\|u\\|_V^2$, hence $u=0$.'), ('Galerkin stability', 'For coercive $a$, the Galerkin solution $u_h$ satisfies $\\|u_h\\|_V\\le c^{-1}\\|F\\|_{V^*}$.', 'Test the discrete equation with $u_h$ and combine coercivity with the dual norm bound.')]}, 'III/24': {'intro': 'A fundamental solution is a distributional inverse kernel for a differential operator. Once one is known, convolution converts a constant-coefficient PDE into an explicit representation formula.', 'sections': [('Definition', 'A fundamental solution $E$ for $P(D)$ satisfies $P(D)E=\\delta_0$ in distributions.'), ('Convolution solution', 'When convolution is legitimate, $u=E*f$ satisfies $P(D)u=f$.'), ('Laplacian kernels', 'The Laplacian has logarithmic fundamental solution in two dimensions and Newtonian power kernels in dimensions at least three.'), ('Heat kernel', 'The heat kernel is the spacetime propagation kernel for the heat operator with causal structure.'), ('Wave kernels', 'Wave fundamental solutions reflect finite propagation and dimension-dependent support geometry.'), ('Distributional verification', 'The singularity at the origin is detected by integrating by parts on punctured domains and passing to a boundary-flux limit.'), ('Nonuniqueness', 'Adding any homogeneous solution to a fundamental solution yields another fundamental solution.'), ('Regularity away from the pole', 'Fundamental solutions are smooth wherever the source point is absent.')], 'theorems': [('Convolution inversion', 'If $P(D)E=\\delta_0$ and the relevant convolutions exist, then $P(D)(E*f)=f$.', 'Constant-coefficient differentiation commutes with convolution, so $P(D)(E*f)=(P(D)E)*f=\\delta_0*f=f$.'), ('Newtonian fundamental solution', 'For $n\\ge3$, a constant multiple of $|x|^{2-n}$ is a fundamental solution of $-\\Delta$.', 'The function is harmonic away from zero. Integrate against a test function on the complement of a small ball, integrate by parts, and choose the constant so the shrinking boundary flux tends to the test value at zero.'), ('Smoothness off the singularity', 'If $P(D)E=\\delta_0$, then $P(D)E=0$ on every open set avoiding the origin.', 'Test functions supported away from zero annihilate $\\delta_0$, so the distribution solves the homogeneous equation there; standard regularity for the explicit classical kernels gives smoothness.')]}, 'III/25': {'intro': 'Green functions adapt fundamental solutions to domains and boundary conditions. They separate the singular response to a point source from a correction chosen to enforce the boundary data.', 'sections': [('Green function definition', 'For a domain operator, $G(x,y)$ solves the differential equation in $x$ with a point source at $y$ and prescribed boundary behavior.'), ('Singular plus regular decomposition', 'Typically $G(x,y)=E(x-y)-H(x,y)$, where $E$ is a whole-space fundamental solution and $H$ corrects the boundary values.'), ('Representation formula', 'Solutions are expressed by integrating the forcing against $G$, plus boundary terms when nonhomogeneous data are present.'), ('Symmetry', 'Self-adjoint elliptic problems often yield $G(x,y)=G(y,x)$.'), ('Positivity', 'Maximum principles can imply nonnegativity of Dirichlet Green functions for positive elliptic operators.'), ('One-dimensional construction', 'For second-order ODEs, Green kernels are assembled from two homogeneous solutions matched continuously with a derivative jump.'), ('Boundary dependence', 'Changing Dirichlet to Neumann conditions changes the correction and may require compatibility conditions.'), ('Operator inverse viewpoint', 'The Green kernel is the integral kernel of the inverse or resolvent of the boundary-value operator.')], 'theorems': [('Green representation', 'If $G$ is a Dirichlet Green function for $L$ and $Lu=f$, then formally $u(x)=\\int_\\Omega G(x,y)f(y)dy$ for homogeneous boundary data.', "Apply Green's identity to $u$ and $G(x,\\cdot)$, use the point-source equation for $G$, and use the boundary condition to eliminate the boundary term."), ('Symmetry for self-adjoint problems', 'Under suitable self-adjoint boundary conditions, $G(x,y)=G(y,x)$.', "Apply Green's identity to the two Green functions with poles at $x$ and $y$, excise small neighborhoods of the poles, and pass to the limit; boundary terms vanish by the shared boundary condition."), ('Derivative jump in one dimension', "For $-u''=f$ on an interval, the Green kernel is continuous across $x=y$ and its first derivative has the jump required to create a Dirac mass.", 'Integrating the equation $-G_{xx}=\\delta_y$ across a small interval around $y$ gives $-(G_x(y^+)-G_x(y^-))=1$, while absence of a delta-prime term forces continuity.')]}, 'III/26': {'intro': 'Sturm--Liouville Green kernels combine boundary-value ODE theory, self-adjointness, Wronskians, and spectral expansions. They are the one-dimensional prototype of resolvent kernels for elliptic operators.', 'sections': [('Sturm--Liouville operator', "Consider $Ly=-(py')'+qy$ with separated boundary conditions and positive weight."), ('Homogeneous fundamental solutions', 'Choose one solution satisfying the left boundary condition and one satisfying the right boundary condition.'), ('Wronskian structure', 'A weighted Wronskian is constant for homogeneous solutions and controls the denominator of the Green kernel.'), ('Piecewise kernel', 'Use one product of homogeneous solutions for $x<\\xi$ and the reversed product for $x>\\xi$.'), ('Matching conditions', 'Continuity at the source and a derivative jump enforce the Dirac equation.'), ('Self-adjoint symmetry', 'Self-adjoint boundary conditions make the Green kernel symmetric.'), ('Eigenfunction expansion', 'Away from eigenvalues, the resolvent kernel can be expanded in normalized eigenfunctions with spectral denominators.'), ('Resonance', 'At an eigenvalue the inverse fails and solvability requires orthogonality compatibility with the nullspace.')], 'theorems': [('Green-kernel construction', 'Let $u$ satisfy the left boundary condition and $v$ the right one. Away from eigenvalues, $G(x,\\xi)$ is proportional to $u(x_<)v(x_>)$ divided by the weighted Wronskian.', 'Each branch solves the homogeneous equation and the boundary condition on its side. Continuity and the prescribed flux jump at $x=\\xi$ determine the common normalization by the Wronskian.'), ('Symmetry', 'For a regular self-adjoint Sturm--Liouville problem, $G(x,\\xi)=G(\\xi,x)$.', 'The product formula is symmetric after swapping $x$ and $\\xi$, or equivalently follows from self-adjointness of the inverse.'), ('Spectral resolvent expansion', 'If $\\{\\phi_n\\}$ is an orthonormal eigenbasis and $\\lambda$ avoids the spectrum, then the Green kernel has formal expansion $G_\\lambda(x,\\xi)=\\sum_n\\phi_n(x)\\phi_n(\\xi)/(\\lambda_n-\\lambda)$.', 'Expand the forcing in the eigenbasis, solve each scalar coefficient equation, and read off the integral kernel of the resulting inverse.')]}, 'III/27': {'intro': 'Maximum principles turn the sign of an elliptic operator into strong qualitative control. They yield uniqueness, comparison, boundary estimates, and positivity of Green functions without explicitly solving the PDE.', 'sections': [('Elliptic operators', 'Second-order ellipticity means the principal coefficient matrix is uniformly positive definite.'), ('Weak maximum principle', 'For suitable operators, a subsolution cannot achieve a strict positive interior maximum beyond its boundary maximum.'), ('Strong maximum principle', 'A nonconstant elliptic solution cannot attain an interior extremum under the standard sign hypotheses.'), ('Comparison principle', 'If $Lu\\le Lv$ and the boundary ordering agrees, then the interior ordering follows.'), ('Uniqueness', 'The maximum principle applied to the difference of two solutions yields uniqueness for Dirichlet problems.'), ('Hopf boundary lemma', 'At a nonconstant boundary extremum, the outward normal derivative has a strict sign under suitable smoothness.'), ('Positivity preservation', 'Nonnegative data often produce nonnegative solutions.'), ('Barriers', 'Explicit super- and subsolutions transfer geometric boundary information into quantitative estimates.')], 'theorems': [('Weak maximum principle', 'If $Lu\\ge0$ for a uniformly elliptic operator with appropriate lower-order signs, then the maximum of $u$ is controlled by the boundary maximum.', 'At a hypothetical strict interior maximum, the gradient vanishes and the Hessian is negative semidefinite; ellipticity makes the principal second-order contribution have the wrong sign, contradicting the differential inequality after the standard perturbation argument.'), ('Comparison principle', 'If $Lu\\ge Lv$ in $\\Omega$ and $u\\le v$ on the boundary, then $u\\le v$ in $\\Omega$ under the maximum-principle hypotheses.', 'Apply the weak maximum principle to $u-v$.'), ('Dirichlet uniqueness', 'A homogeneous Dirichlet solution of $Lu=0$ is identically zero under the maximum-principle hypotheses.', 'Apply the maximum principle to $u$ and to $-u$ to show both maximum and minimum are zero.')]}, 'III/28': {'intro': 'Spectral and transform methods solve linear PDEs by diagonalizing the operator. Fourier transforms diagonalize translation-invariant operators, while eigenfunction expansions diagonalize boundary-value operators on bounded domains.', 'sections': [('Symbol of an operator', 'A constant-coefficient differential operator becomes multiplication by its polynomial symbol under the Fourier transform.'), ('Elliptic multipliers', 'Solving $P(D)u=f$ in frequency space amounts to dividing by the symbol where the division is meaningful.'), ('Heat equation', 'The heat transform multiplier $e^{-4\\pi^2t|\\xi|^2}$ gives smoothing and decay.'), ('Wave equation', 'Frequency modes oscillate with phase determined by the dispersion relation.'), ('Poisson equation', 'The Fourier multiplier $|\\xi|^{-2}$ represents the Newtonian potential, interpreted distributionally at low frequency.'), ('Eigenfunction expansions', 'On bounded domains, self-adjoint elliptic operators admit orthogonal eigenfunctions that replace Fourier exponentials.'), ('Semigroups', 'Spectral calculus defines $e^{-tL}$ and links PDE evolution to functional calculus.'), ('Boundary and whole-space synthesis', 'Transform methods exploit translation symmetry; spectral methods adapt the same diagonalization idea to domains and boundary conditions.')], 'theorems': [('Heat transform solution', 'For $u_t=\\Delta u$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ with initial data $u_0$, $\\widehat u(t,\\xi)=e^{-4\\pi^2t|\\xi|^2}\\widehat u_0(\\xi)$.', 'Fourier transform converts the PDE into the scalar ODE $\\partial_t\\widehat u=-4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2\\widehat u$ for each frequency; solve the ODE with the transformed initial data.'), ('Spectral heat expansion', 'If $L\\phi_k=\\lambda_k\\phi_k$ on a bounded self-adjoint problem, then $u(t)=\\sum_ke^{-t\\lambda_k}\\langle u_0,\\phi_k\\rangle\\phi_k$ solves $u_t+Lu=0$.', "Expand the initial data in the eigenbasis; each coefficient satisfies the scalar ODE $c_k'+\\lambda_kc_k=0$."), ('Resolvent diagonalization', 'For $\\lambda$ off the spectrum, $(L-\\lambda)^{-1}$ multiplies each eigenfunction coefficient by $(\\lambda_k-\\lambda)^{-1}$.', 'Apply $L-\\lambda$ to the eigenfunction expansion and solve the scalar coefficient equations independently.')]}}
